 freelance nature of music industry  if they can't get any support they will  get jobs elsewhere  so even if we do go back to playing  they won't necessarily be necessarily be  a workforce  force left to actually support those  gigs but as an ambassador of the music  venue trust  i am also privy to the fact that 97  percent of grassroots venues  will not be able to stay open with any  kind of social  distancing and and the only way of  avoiding that  is some government assistance i wonder  whether part of the problem  might well be that for for many people  perhaps those who are not regular  concert  gig goers they tend to think of the o2  the manchester arena these  massive venues that exist but actually  fundamentally  what is the backbone of the british  music industry as you've already  mentioned  are those smaller places that get you  out there in front of the public and get  you the experience  the launch pads and their their labors  of love  these venues you know they're constantly  up against rising business taxes they're  constantly  up against developers trying to buy the  properties and turn them into flats  and they're just to be honest a lot of  them are hanging on by the skin of their  teeth  anyway regardless of the pandemic and  and what has  you know happened because of that so  it's really it's crunch time i've heard  people talking about this in terms of  live music as an extinction level event  and it sounds dramatic  it really is this is a really dramatic  uh potential future for live music  and really we've got to look at  um at how important  live music and the experience of live  music  is to our country because  it's really in peril right now  um how is lockdown being then for for a  musician  like yourself i mean have you been able  to collaborate with people we've seen  plenty of video conference bands coming  together i mean has that been your  experience  i found it pretty hard to be creative  during this time i managed to do a  charity 